# Factoring and design

## Small words

A colon definition should do **one recognizable thing** at the level of abstraction of its name. If you need inline comments to explain every line, extract words.

```forth
\ BAD — monolith, no named steps, magic 255
: send-packet ( addr u -- )
  dup 255 min over c! 1+ swap 1- ... ;

\ GOOD — named steps, each with a clear stack effect
255 constant max-payload
: clamp-payload ( u -- u' ) max-payload min ;
: store-byte    ( addr c -- addr+1 ) over c! 1+ ;
: advance       ( addr u -- addr+1 u-1 ) 1 /string ;
```

## Bottom-up

Build a **vocabulary** for the problem domain first (low-level, tested words), then compose application words. The language of words should read like the problem statement.

## Rule of three

If the same sequence of three or more words appears twice, factor it into a new word unless inlining is required for speed (document that exception).

## Defer optimization

Write clear factored code first. Merge or specialize only after profiling on the target system. Inlining via `compile,` / `postpone` is an advanced, dialect-specific step — do not default to it.

## Component boundaries

Group related words in one file or block. List **exports** (words other modules may use) at the top of the file comment. Hide helpers (no external documentation) when the project supports it.

## Tests at the keyboard

New words should be runnable in isolation at the interpreter with a documented stack setup:

```forth
\ setup -- try -- cleanup
s" test" 2constant test$
test$ count type cr
```
